# Strong interview answer

> **I would deploy multiple LoRA adapters by serving one shared base model and selecting the LoRA adapter per request. Each adapter would be versioned and registered in an adapter registry containing the tenant, base-model compatibility, storage location, version, and deployment status.**
>
> **The API layer would authenticate the user, determine the tenant, and resolve the authorized adapter server-side rather than allowing users to provide arbitrary adapter paths. Redis would cache tenant-to-adapter mappings, while PostgreSQL would be the source of truth.**
>
> **For inference, I would use an engine such as vLLM with LoRA enabled. Frequently used adapters would remain warm in GPU memory, less frequently used adapters could be cached on CPU, and cold adapters would live in object storage. I would configure concurrency and maximum LoRA rank based on actual adapter characteristics.**
>
> **For scale, I would deploy multiple inference replicas on Kubernetes, add request routing, per-tenant rate limits, adapter-aware caching, monitoring, and autoscaling. Adapter deployments would use immutable versions with canary releases and fast rollback through the adapter registry. I would also isolate adapter management because runtime loading from untrusted paths introduces security risks.** ([vLLM][1])

## The one-line answer to remember

```text
Load the base model once,
share it across tenants,
and dynamically select the
correct versioned LoRA adapter per request.
```

